description:
pkix-truststore/src/lib.rs:376–380 — `from_der_iter` builds its output Vec with:

    let mut anchors = Vec::new();
    for (i, bytes) in iter.into_iter().enumerate() {
        let cert = Certificate::from_der(bytes.as_ref()).map_err(|_| Error::MalformedAnchor(i))?;
        anchors.push(TrustAnchor::from_cert(cert));
    }

This is the exact pattern the rusty review prompt enumerates: `for` loops that accumulate into a `Vec` via `.push()` instead of `.map()` + `.collect()`. The idiomatic equivalent:

    let anchors: Vec<TrustAnchor> = iter
        .into_iter()
        .enumerate()
        .map(|(i, bytes)| {
            Certificate::from_der(bytes.as_ref())
                .map_err(|_| Error::MalformedAnchor(i))
                .map(TrustAnchor::from_cert)
        })
        .collect::<Result<_, _>>()?;

What goes wrong if it stays:
1. `.collect::<Result<Vec<_>, _>>()` preallocates the Vec using the iterator's `size_hint()`; the explicit `Vec::new()` does not, so well-behaved upstream iterators (slices, Vecs, ExactSizeIterator) miss a free preallocation. `from_der_iter` is the canonical adapter entry point that will be hit by every system-store / PKCS#11 / KMS adapter — preallocation matters at scale.
2. Intent is less clear: the `for`+`?` form mixes 'transform every item' with 'propagate first error' as imperative steps; collect-into-Result expresses both as one operation.
3. Inconsistency with the rest of the crate (`Ok(certs.into_iter().map(TrustAnchor::from_cert).collect())` at line 334 in `from_pem` uses the collect form already).
